        "text": "Subsecciones de: Chapter 5: Testing and Automation with Python\nP\u00e1ginas: 174-207\n\u2022 Technical requirements  (175)\n\u2022 Understanding various levels of testing  (175)\n  \u2022 Unit testing  (176)\n  \u2022 Integration testing  (177)\n  \u2022 System testing  (177)\n  \u2022 Acceptance testing  (178)\n\u2022 Working with Python test frameworks  (178)\n  \u2022 Working with the unittest framework  (180)\n  \u2022 Working with the pytest framework  (192)\n\u2022 Executing TDD  (202)\n  \u2022 Red  (202)\n  \u2022 Green  (202)\n  \u2022 Refactor  (203)\n\u2022 Introducing automated CI  (204)\n\u2022 Summary  (205)\n\u2022 Questions  (205)\n\u2022 Further reading  (205)\n\u2022 Answers  (206)\n5\nTesting and\nAutomation\nwith Python\nSoftware testing is the process of validating an application or a program as per user\nrequirements or desired specifications and evaluating the software for scalability and\noptimization goals. Validating software as a real user takes a long time and is not an\nefficient use of human resources. Moreover, testing is not performed only one or two\ntimes, but it is a continuous process as a part of software development. To rescue the\nsituation, test automation is recommended for all sorts of testing. Test automation is a set\nof programs written to validate an application's behavior using different scenarios as input\nto these programs. For professional software development environments, it is a must that\nautomation tests get executed every time the source code is updated (also called a commit\noperation ) into a central repository.\n152     Testing and Automation with Python\nIn this chapter, we will study different approaches to automated testing, followed by\nlooking at different types of testing frameworks and libraries that are available for Python\napplications. Then, we will focus on unit testing and will look into different ways of\nimplementing unit testing in Python. Next, we will study the usefulness of test-driven\ndevelopment ( TDD ) and the right way to implement it. Finally, we will focus on automated\ncontinuous integration ( CI ) and will look into the challenges of implementing it robustly",
